* ALDO commands accessible from the lisp REPL.

They are defined in a thin interactor pushed below the lisp interactor
in the lisp REPL only; the same commands are accessible from the usual
aldo interactor when aldo is active.

This implies that if commands with the same name are defined in the
lisp interactor, the command defined in the lisp interactor shadows
the debugging commands. As always, we can access the interactor below
by prefixing the command with the interactor name or nickname, ALDO or
DEBUG.

                                              
| short | command              | argument   | description                                            |
|-------+----------------------+------------+--------------------------------------------------------|
| b     | break                | file:linum |                                                        |
| b     | break                | linum      | (assuming we have a current file)                      |
| b     | break                | func-name  | breaks on function entry                               |
| lb    | list breakpoints     | ∅          | list break points                                      |
| eb    | enable breakpoint    | ppN        | enable a breakpoint                                    |
| eb    | enable breakpoint    | ALL        | enable all breakpoints                                 |
| db    | disable breakpoint   | ppN        | disable a breakpoint                                   |
| db    | disable breakpoint   | ALL        | disable all breakpoints                                |
| xb    | delete breakpoint    | ppN        | delete a breakpoint                                    |
| xb    | delete breakpoint    | ALL        | delete all breakpoints (ask confirmation).             |
| cb    | condition breakpoint | ppN sexp   | add a condition to the breakpoint                      |
| ib    | ignore breakpoint    | ppN count  | ignore the next count executions of the breakpoint     |
|-------+----------------------+------------+--------------------------------------------------------|
| ls    | list source          | func-name  | pretty print the function source                       |
| ls    | list source          | file       | list the file source thru configured pager.            |
|       |                      |            | In both case: prints with breakpoint, etc, indicators, |
|       |                      |            | and set the current file.                              |
|-------+----------------------+------------+--------------------------------------------------------|

* New LISP REPL Command

We have SET and WRITE-SETTING commands in the Lisp REPL command set,
We are missing a SETTINGS command to print out the current settings.


We'll also add an inspect command that does the same as
=(inspector form)= or =(clal-inspect form)=.

| short | command  | argument | description                                  |
|-------+----------+----------+----------------------------------------------|
|       | settings | ∅        | Prints the current settings (hierarchically, |
|       |          |          | showing the various cascading levels         |
|-------+----------+----------+----------------------------------------------|
| i     | inspect  | form     | inspect the result of evaluating form.       |
|-------+----------+----------+----------------------------------------------|

* PAGER option

The lisp configuration can contain a pager settings as does the aldo configuration
In the lisp repl, the lisp configuration takes over.
In the aldo debugger, the aldo configuration takes over.
(Just the normal cascading configuration paralleling the interactor stack).

There are two pager options:

- (PAGER . ON)
- (PAGER-HEIGHT . 24)

However the PAGER setting can be ON, OFF, or also a namestring, ie. a
string containing the path to a pager filter like =more(1)=,
=less(1)=, etc.

